@node	0.6, 0.7, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Records, mutable fields, and refs

@noindent
A record groups named fields into one value, the way this book otherwise
uses a list of two elements or a small variant, but with each field
addressed by name rather than by position:

@example
# type point = @{ x : float; y : float @};;
type point = @{ x : float; y : float; @}
# let origin = @{ x = 0.0; y = 0.0 @};;
val origin : point = @{x = 0.; y = 0.@}
@end example

@noindent
By default every field of a record is immutable: once built, a @code{point}
cannot change. ``Changing'' one field of an immutable record means building
a new record that copies every other field, which the @code{with} syntax
does directly:

@example
# let right_three = @{ origin with x = 3.0 @};;
val right_three : point = @{x = 3.; y = 0.@}
# right_three;;
- : point = @{x = 3.; y = 0.@}
# origin;;
- : point = @{x = 0.; y = 0.@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{origin} is unchanged; @code{right_three} is a new, separate value that
happens to share @code{origin}'s @code{y}. This is exactly the same
distinction Scheme draws between building a new list with @code{cons} and
mutating an existing one with @code{set-car!}: functional update copies,
and the original is never at risk.

@subsubheading Mutable fields

A field declared @code{mutable} can be reassigned in place with @code{<-},
without building a new record:

@example
# type counter = @{ mutable count : int @};;
type counter = @{ mutable count : int; @}
# let fresh_counter () = @{ count = 0 @};;
val fresh_counter : unit -> counter = <fun>
# let bump counter = counter.count <- counter.count + 1;;
val bump : counter -> unit = <fun>
# let counter = fresh_counter ();;
val counter : counter = @{count = 0@}
# bump counter;;
- : unit = ()
# bump counter;;
- : unit = ()
# counter.count;;
- : int = 2
@end example

@noindent
@code{bump}'s return type is @code{unit}: it is called only for the field
assignment it performs, not for any value it produces. This is this book's
smallest example of a stateful object in the sense chapter 3 develops at length: a value with an identity that persists across mutations, as opposed to a plain value.

@subsubheading @code{ref}: a mutable cell holding one value

A one-field mutable record is common enough that the standard library
predefines it: @code{'a ref} is exactly @code{@{ mutable contents : 'a @}}
under a shorter name, built with @code{ref}, read with @code{!}, and
assigned with @code{:=}:

@example
# let after_deposit () =
    let balance = ref 100 in
    balance := !balance + 20;
    !balance;;
val after_deposit : unit -> int = <fun>
# after_deposit ();;
- : int = 120
@end example

@noindent
This is this edition's translation of Scheme's mutable local state, most
directly the pattern chapter 3's @code{make-withdraw} and
@code{make-account} use: a value captured by @code{ref} that a closure can
read and rewrite between calls. Section 0.7 builds exactly that example once
closures are introduced.

@subsubheading Aliasing: two names, one cell

Binding a second name to an existing @code{ref} does not copy the cell; both
names refer to the same mutable location, and a write through either name is
visible through the other:

@example
# let alias_cell () =
    let cell = ref 10 in
    let alias = cell in
    alias := !alias + 5;
    !cell;;
val alias_cell : unit -> int = <fun>
# alias_cell ();;
- : int = 15
@end example

@noindent
@code{alias} was never told to update @code{cell}; @code{alias} @emph{is}
@code{cell}, under a second name. This is @newterm{aliasing}, and it is the
one behavior a purely functional update, like the record @code{with} syntax
from the start of this section, can never produce:

@example
# let copy_point () =
    let original = @{ x = 1.0; y = 1.0 @} in
    let moved = @{ original with x = 9.0 @} in
    (original.x, moved.x);;
val copy_point : unit -> float * float = <fun>
# copy_point ();;
- : float * float = (1., 9.)
@end example

@noindent
@code{moved} shares no mutable state with @code{original}: moving it left
@code{original} exactly where it was. Whether a value can alias is a
property of the value's type, not of how carefully a program is written:
records with a @code{ref} field, or a @code{ref} itself, can be aliased;
ordinary immutable records, tuples, and lists cannot, because nothing about
them is assignable in the first place.

@subsubheading Physical equality distinguishes aliasing from mere equal contents

@code{==} tests whether two values are the very same mutable cell in
memory, as opposed to @code{=}, which tests whether their contents happen to
match:

@example
# let cell_identity () =
    let a = ref 1 in
    let b = ref 1 in
    let alias = a in
    (a == b, a == alias);;
val cell_identity : unit -> bool * bool = <fun>
# cell_identity ();;
- : bool * bool = (false, true)
@end example

@noindent
@code{a} and @code{b} hold equal contents (both @code{1}) but are two
distinct cells, so @code{a == b} is @code{false}; @code{alias} is not a copy
of @code{a} but another name for the very same cell, so @code{a == alias}
is @code{true}. This is this edition's @code{==}, corresponding to Scheme's
@code{eq?}; it matters only for mutable data (@code{ref}s and
mutable-field records), since two immutable values with equal contents are,
for every purpose a program can observe, interchangeable.
